### Recharter Discussion
- **Presentation:** [03 Recharter Discussion](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-teas-sessb-03-recharter-discussion-00) (Pawan Goyal, Oscar González de Dios)
- The WG charter was last updated in 2018. Rechartering is proposed to reflect active work items and account for new scope:
  - Explicit deliverables for Network Slicing and Network Resource Partitions (NRPs).
  - Abstraction and Control of Traffic Engineered Networks (ACTN) applicability.
  - RSVP-TE hardening, security, and scalability.
  - Power-aware traffic engineering (`draft-ietf-teas-power-steering`) and multi-path TE.
  - Updated relationships with working groups (ONSEN, NMOP, GREEN, TSVWG, SRv6ops) and external organizations (3GPP, O-RAN).
- **Discussion:**
  - Kireeti Kompella noted that much of the existing work (IP, MPLS, Segment Routing) falls under the current charter umbrella, but milestone updates and group coordination references are necessary.
  - Jim Guichard (AD) supported updating the charter to streamline IESG evaluation of WG documents.
  - In chat, Ran Atkinson, Adrian Farrel, Vishnu Beeram, and Jie Dong discussed broadening RSVP security text to encompass general Traffic Engineering security risks and centralized TE control security.
  - Swamy Senapathi inquired about the boundary between TEAS and ONSEN regarding network slicing. Oscar González de Dios and Pawan Goyal clarified that service models and high-level abstractions may fall under ONSEN, whereas TE-specific slicing realization mechanisms remain in TEAS.

### Applicability of Data Models for Network Slice Service Management
- **Presentation:** [04 Applicability of IETF-Defined Service and Network Data Models for Network Slice Service Management](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-teas-sessb-04-applicability-of-ietf-defined-service-and-network-data-models-for-network-slice-service-management-00) (Victor Lopez)
- **Draft:** `draft-ietf-teas-ns-models-applicability`
- Victor Lopez presented updates in revision -03:
  - Abstract was condensed and readability improved.
  - Section 5.3 highlights gaps where information mapping requires slice controller logic (e.g., slice-level monitoring configuration frequency).
  - The multi-domain analysis section was retained due to lack of objections.
- Technical content is stable, and authors requested moving the draft toward WGLC.

### ACTN POI Assurance
- **Presentation:** [05 ACTN POI Assurance](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-teas-sessb-05-actn-poi-assurance-00) (Paolo Belotti)
- **Draft:** `draft-ietf-teas-actn-poi-assurance`
- Paolo Belotti presented updates from pull request #37, which reduced the scope of the IP layer analysis to a single IP domain over optical networks (multi-layer, single administrative IP domain).
- Correlating end-to-end assurance across multiple IP domains was deemed excessively complex for this draft and will be suggested for separate future work. End-to-end VPN performance monitoring remains supported.
- Authors will complete final section reviews (fault analysis, protection, performance monitoring) and candidate YANG model references before requesting WGLC.

### ACTN Extensions for Inter-Operator Coordination
- **Presentation:** [06 ACTN Extensions for Inter-Operator Coordination](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-teas-sessb-06-actn-extensions-for-inter-operator-coordination-00) (Taki- Yessica)
- Taki- Yessica presented updates on inter-operator MDSC coordination using the Multi-MDSC Interface (MMI) within the ACTN framework.
- Revision -01 adds Section 4.2, highlighting technical gaps when applying the Virtual Network (VN) recursion model (RFC 8453) to inter-operator scenarios:
  1. Agreement on boundary link TE resources (wavelengths, MPLS labels, SR SIDs).
  2. End-to-end TE constraint validation (latency, optical OSNR, optical impairments).
- **Discussion:**
  - In chat, Dhruv Dhody and Adrian Farrel discussed RFC 8453 architectural model assumptions regarding hierarchical vs. peer MDSC relationships.
  - Italo Buzzi questioned operator willingness to expose internal/vendor-specific TE resource details across operator boundaries and noted multi-vendor optical path computation complexity.
  - Oscar González de Dios noted applicability in cooperative/friendly inter-operator scenarios (such as regional entities within the same parent enterprise) and recommended presenting pure optical aspects in the CCAMP WG.
  - Paolo Belotti and Swamy Senapathi suggested exploring abstracted objects or parameter subsets to limit sensitive data exposure.

